Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:06.389 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this week on the dish  an immigrant success story born in  lebanon chef eddie massey says  he'd like to be the middle eastern  martha stewart at just 26 he appears to  be on his way  from food to layout massey does it all  it started with a creative spin on  mediterranean apps from hummus to baba  ganoush to stuffed grape leaves  now it's his latest venture launched in  the midst of the pandemic  in his very own green point brooklyn new  york neighborhood  when i first moved in it just felt like  a little poland in a way  eddie massey has watched firsthand as  his once predominantly polish  brooklyn neighborhood has diversified a  prime example  his self-named store standing here in  this neighborhood  and seeing your sign your place what  does this mean to you  it means a lot to me i mean it's crazy  to think that i came from a small  fishing village in the north side of  lebanon to this now  in the middle of one of the biggest  cities in the world inside eddie's  grocer  a taste of his homeland including an  array of middle eastern spreads  side dishes and traditional lebanese  entrees  all with massey's own spin you've got  the rizage right there  which is a lebanese dirty rice right  here  is what we call it it's a chickpea and  eggplant stew  it was some of his favorites we tasted  here we've got zatar chicken which is my  little invention  as massey shared his journey to the  united states from lebanon  at age 10. my parents wanted us to come  my sister and i did not want to come  that's for sure  we loved our life and i think at that  age it's a very hard  move especially because we didn't speak  the language massey admits  it was work learning english and just  fitting  in like in the school cafeteria which  he'd  only known from the american tv show  lizzie mcguire  what are you eating peanut butter and  banana sandwich  showing up to a cafeteria with a labnae  tartine  and like lebanese bread rolled up and  kids being like  what are you eating hummus wasn't a  thing like it is now right  where somebody would look over my  shoulder be like you're eating poop that  looks like poop and you're like oh god  but at home that same food was a part of  the fabric of his family  food is like the masterpiece of our life  in lebanon i think every  day all you do is join your family  around a big meal  and you're always talking about the next  meal where are we eating what are we  cooking  so with both of his parents working  massey took over in the family kitchen  by high school he started to see a  future in food  launching a cooking club and creating  his own cooking show  airing on local cable where were your  parents in  being okay with all of this because  sometimes parents have other thoughts of  what our future is  you they did my dad was very supportive  because my dad loves to eat it's like  his favorite thing so  he he was very supportive and i think  that his dad made him do  something else that he wanted to do so  he was like you do what you want my mom  did not want me to cook what did she  want you to do  she just just not gross anything but  cook  he enrolled in the culinary institute of  america anyway  but it was his suspension from it for  plagiarism  that gave massey direction that's when  he got a job at famed restaurateur danny  meyer's  north end grill i say this all the time  but you can skip culinary school and go  work for danny meyer  the way his system is done and how  everything is so well thought out  in front of the house back in the house  did you have a desire  to sort of finish what you had started  though everybody that i was working with  all my managers were  culinary street america graduates so  they helped me get back to school  massey finished his degree while working  for meyer  then got out of the restaurant business  and into people's homes in 2016 he began  catering middle eastern spreads and  meals that were as appetizing to the  eyes as the stomach  he quickly gained notoriety for his  platters known as  brown paper boards thanks to their  signature look  when i started i was praying for like  one to two events a week  right like literally i was like i hope i  can get one dinner  and the really height of it all i  remember one week i did 22 events  then last march kovit brought his  catering business to a halt  massey tried doing delivery out of his  own kitchen  but hit a snag the new york post heard  about me  they wrote me up which was amazing my  sales tripled that week  and then right after that the health  department called and was like this is a  no-no  and they shut me down over the phone and  i was like well what am i going to do  now  this corner store was the answer his  go-to sandwich shop maria's deli  owner maria puke had moved to the  neighborhood from poland  when she was 10. she worked as a  kid in the grocery store and she used to  hand deliver groceries and stock it  and then she took it over when she  turned  22. wow she has been working and running  that deli for 43 years until massey took  over  last july he leased the space from maria  kept touches of her within  and in the midst of the pandemic  transformed it into eddie's grocer  in just six weeks where do you get  the energy the brain power the vision  where does all that come from  cold brew you know my parents sacrificed  a lot for us when we moved here they  gave up a lot we  lived a really great lifestyle in  lebanon  and they gave it all up for our future  and i think that  every day work hard to prove to them  that they did it for a reason  like his chosen career believing if his  family hadn't moved to the u.s  he wouldn't be a chef and that as a gay  man in a small town in lebanon  he probably never would have come out  you're a lebanese-american  gay chef it's a lot of labels but  certainly diverse  and unique i am the voice of those  little boys in that town and  those little boys in all the towns of  lebanon to be able to  jump that leap and be proud of who i am  it's something that  they might never experience as for  massey's next leap  oh god this is your life eddie yeah i  know  i'm trying to figure that question out  do i know where i'm going  no am i happy with where we're going yes  also happy his parents including his mom  you may remember didn't want him to be a  chef  he says they're even thinking about  moving from massachusetts to new york  city so they can help him  with the store and i couldn't leave my  cohorts  hanging this is like a mini version of  these brown paper boards with some of  the spreads  absolutely delicious and really works of  art  the color is just amazing  do not be surprised if you see eddie  massey one day on your tv screen  with all of this that's great do we know  what all these i actually don't know  what he sent to us and because we can't  reach i mean obviously there's some  olives there and  um i think some tabbouleh and that mix  as well so basically we can't  we're gonna dive in we are gonna dive in  here oh there you go okay

